Reactive metal–ligand (M–L) multiply bonded complexes are critical intermediates in many biological C–H functionalization reactions. Intermediate Q, a diiron(IV) bis- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\upmu}$ -oxo complex embedded in the active site of soluble methane monooxygenase (sMMO), is responsible for partial oxidation of methane to methanol in methanotrophic bacteria (Figure 1).1 Soluble MMO achieves selective partial methane oxidation by conformationally gated substrate diffusion to, and product egress from, the enzyme active site.2 Analysis of kinetic isotope effects (KIEs), which measure the impact of isotopic substitution on reaction rates, can provide direct evidence of conformationally gated diffusion. For example, sMMO catalyzed $C-H$ hydroxylation often exhibits large intramolecular KIEs (i.e. $k_{\mathsf{H}}/k_{\mathsf{D}}=4.2,$ 3 measured using partially deuterated ethane, where $k_{\mathsf{H}}/k_{\mathsf{D}}$ is the ratio of the rates of $C-H$ and C–D functionalization) but negligible intermolecular KIEs (i.e. $k_{\mathsf{H}}/k_{\mathsf{D}}=~1.0,$ 4 measured using mixtures of perdeuterated and undeuterated substrates).5 The combination of large intramolecular KIEs and small intermolecular isotope effects has been interpreted to indicate that while $C-H$ cleavage is involved in hydroxylation, diffusional barriers, which are not sensitive to isotopic substitution, are rate determining. Similar experimental data indicate that substrate access to compound I, the oxoiron intermediate in cytochrome P450s,6 can also be diffusionally limited.
In the absence of a protein superstructure, reactive M–L multiply bonded complexes can participate in a variety of decomposition reactions, such as ligand oxidation chemistry, bimolecular dimerization, and solvent oxidation reactions, which prevent utilization of these reactive structures for intermolecular C–H functionalization reactions. 7  For example, diruthenium nitride 2 participates in facile intramolecular $C-H$ amination of proximal ligand-based C–H bonds to afford amido complex 3 (Figure 2a). 8  Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are porous materials based on metal ions and organic linking groups have been advanced as catalysis platforms based on catalyst site isolation and because the pore space provides an opportunity to co-localize substrates in proximity to catalyst sites.9 In order to leverage many of the unique opportunities of MOFs in catalysis, substrates must diffuse to interstitial latticeconfined catalyst sites. While in many cases indirect evidence is available for the impact of diffusion on substrate functionalization, 10  experimental tools to directly evaluate substrate diffusion through porous materials during interstitial chemistry are lacking.
We were attracted to the hypothesis that confinement of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ nitride intermediates that are isoelectronic to 2 within porous coordination lattices could enable intermolecular C–H amination. Development of amination via lattice-confined carboxylatesupported $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ sites was guided by the following design principles: 1) M–M cooperation in a Ru2 nitride intermediate would provide access to highly reactive $M-L$ multiply bonded intermediates, 11 2) unlike the formamidinate ligands that are used to stabilize 2, carboxylate ligands would not present ligandbased C–H bonds in proximity to reactive $M-L$ multiple bonds, 3) confinement of reactive intermediates12 as structural elements of a MOF lattice would suppress bimolecular nitride coupling reactions,13 and 4) material porosity could co-localize substrate in proximity to the reactive $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ nitride to facilitate intermolecular a) Intramolecular C-H amination with molecular Ru2 nitride nitrogen-atom transfer (NAT). Here, we report the intermolecular amination of toluene at a $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ nitride intermediate confined as part of a MOF lattice (Figure 2b). Further, using the relative magnitudes of intra- and intermolecular KIEs for toluene amination, we demonstrate that substrate diffusion in the developed porous material is slower than $C{\-}\dot{\mathsf{H}}$ amination. We anticipate the developed kinetic tools will be useful to the ongoing development of catalysis with porous materials.
We initiated our examination of the potential for latticeconfined intermolecular group transfer chemistry with $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ , a porous MOF comprised of Ru2[II,III] nodes and 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylate $(\mathsf{b t c}^{3-})$ linkers. 14 $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ is isomorphous with Cu3btc2 (HKUST-1).15 M3btc2 networks display a 3-dimensional channel structure and feature three distinct pore environments.16 The smallest pore displays an $8.1\mathrm{~\AA~}$ M-to-M separation and is accessed via a $4.0\mathsf{A}$ aperture (M–M distance minus van der Waals radii of Ru). The two larger pores feature M-to-M separation greater than $11.4\mathbb{A}$ and are accessed by a $7.3\mathsf{A}$ aperture (M–M distance minus van der Waals radii of Ru). In situ $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction studies have demonstrated that while small guests, such as methanol (kinetic diameter: $3.7\mathrm{~\AA~}^{17}.$ ), can access all three pores, larger guests, such as toluene (kinetic diameter: $5.9~\mathsf{A}^{17})$ only access the two larger pores (for additional discussion of M3btc2 structures, see Figure S1).16
Solvothermal combination of ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O A c})_{4}{\sf C I}$ and H3btc in $H_{2}O$ and AcOH at $160^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ affords $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (Figure 3a).14a Powder $x.$ ray diffraction (PXRD) of the as-synthesized material indicates that the obtained network is isostructural to $\mathsf{C u}_{3}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c}_{2}$ (HKUST-1, Figure 3b, see Figure S2 for comparison of experimental and computed PXRD patterns). Elemental analysis of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ indicates a 2:1 mixture of Cl and OH ligands on the $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ sites, which is consistent with previous reports of this material (empirical formula: $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{2}\mathsf{O H}])$ ).14a ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption experiments indicate a BET surface area of $746~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ (Langmuir surface area of $1056~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ , Figure 3c (black)), which is in good agreement with the literature (BET surface area $\l=704\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/9\$ and Langmuir surface area $=891~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ ).14a
${\sf N}_{3}$ -for-Cl exchange is accomplished by soaking $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ in an aqueous $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) solution of $N a N_{3}$ . Exchange of chloride in preference to hydroxide at $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ lattice sites is evidenced by elemental analysis, which is consistent with a $\mathsf{[R u_{6}b t c_{4}C l_{0.26}(N_{3})_{1.44}(O H)_{1.3}]}$ formulation, and may arise from the relative bond dissociation energies of $R\mathsf{u}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l}$ and $\mathsf{R u\mathrm{-OH}}$ bonds. 18  In an alternative synthetic route, $\textsf{a}$ material with empirical formula $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ was accessed by  treatment of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ first with trimethylsilyl triflate (TMSOTf) $\mathsf{i n C H}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ followed by $N a N_{3}$ in an aqueous DMF solution. The azide-exchanged materials obtained from these two procedures displayed identical amination chemistry (vide infra). Prior to subsequent use, the extent of azide exchange in each batch was assessed by elemental analysis. The IR spectrum of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ displays an isotope-sensitive signal at $2048(m^{-1}$ , which shifts to 2031 cm-1 in $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{\Omega}^{15}\mathsf{N N}_{2})_{3}]$ (prepared using $\mathsf{N a}^{15}\mathsf{N N}_{2}$ , Figure 3d). Ligand exchange is accompanied by a bathochromic shift of the solidstate UV-vis absorption features, which is similar to UV-vis spectral changes observed in $N_{3}$ -for-Cl ligand exchange in the synthesis of compound 1 (Figure S3). $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ is isomorphous to $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ based on PXRD (Figure 3b). Extensive soaking of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ in MeOH followed by $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ allowed for low-temperature activation of this material and determination of a BET surface area of $619\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathfrak{g}$ (Figure 3c (red), see Figure S4 and Table S1 for additional discussion of the activation of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}])$ . For comparison, activation of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ under identical low-temperature conditions yielded a BET surface area of $662~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ (Figure 3c (blue)). X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) and magnetometry data collected for $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ indicate that the $s=3/2$ Ru2[II,III] sites in $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ are maintained following ion exchange (Figures S5 and S6, respectively).
To investigate potential group-transfer chemistry, we introduced toluene $(P h C H_{3}$ ; bond dissociation energy (BDE) of $\mathsf{P h C H}_{2-\mathsf{H}}$ is $90~\mathsf{k c a l/m o l}^{19})$ ) into the porous material by soaking $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ in ${\mathsf{P h C H}}_{3}$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ . Heating the ${P h C H}_{3}$ -impregnated material $(P h C H_{3}@[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}])$ to $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ resulted in the disappearance of the azide stretching mode $(2048~\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ in the IR spectrum (Figure S7). Following acidic work-up, benzylamine was observed in $32\%$ yield based on available ${\mathsf{R u}}_{2}{\mathsf{N}}_{3}$ sites. We speculate that the low yield may be due to inefficient substrate diffusion to all available ${\mathsf{R u}}_{2}{\mathsf{N}}_{3}$ sites, for example, due to size exclusion from the smallest pores in $M_{3}b\mathsf{t c}_{2}$ .16 The generated benzylamine was characterized by gas chromatography (GC) co-injection (Figure S8), $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR (Figure S9), and electrospray ionization-mass spectrometry (ESI-MS; $\begin{array}{c c l}{m/z}&{=}&{108.0805.}\end{array}$ calculated $m/z=108.0808$ $(P h C H_{2}N H_{3}^{+})$ ; Figure S10). Based on PXRD (Figure S11) and gas sorption (Figure S12) analyses of the spent $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ -based material, framework crystallinity and porosity are maintained following thermolysis.
To confirm that intermolecular amination of toluene had occurred, we carried out a series of isotope labeling experiments. First, using $d_{8}$ -toluene resulted in the observation of $d_{7^{-}}$ benzylamine $(m/z~=~115.1246$ , calculated $m/z=115.1247$ $(C_{6}\mathsf{D}_{5}\mathsf{C D}_{2}\mathsf{N H}_{3}^{+})\}$ ). Second, amination of toluene using $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{\Omega}^{15}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{N}_{2})_{3}]$ led to a 1:1 mixture of ${\sqrt{4}}\mathsf{N}.$ - and $^{15}N-$ benzylamine, which is expected because $^{15}\mathsf{N N}_{2}$ could bind via either the labeled or the unlabeled terminus (ESI-MS data is summarized in Figure S10). Control experiments in which the $N a N_{3}$ soaking solution was decanted and heated in the presence of toluene resulted in no benzylamine, which confirms the observed amination chemistry proceeds in the solid state. Observation of benzylamine and the described isotope-labeling experiments confirm the viability of intermolecular group transfer chemistry with the developed $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ -based materials.
Cyclohexane and benzene do not participate in amination with $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}(\mathsf{N}_{3})_{3}]$ , which may be due to the C–H bond strength of these substrates (BDE: $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{11}\mathrm{-}H,96\ k c a l/m o l;^{20}C_{6}H_{5}\mathrm{-}H,}$ 113 $\mathsf{k c a l/m o l}^{19})$ . Ortho-xylene is a competent substrate for amination $(\sim5\%$ yield). Meta- and para-xylene do not participate in amination, which we hypothesize may be due to restricted diffusion into the porous $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ material (vide infra).
Unlike lattice-isolated $\mathsf{R u}_{2}[\mathsf{I I I}$ ,III] sites, analogous $\mathsf{R u}_{2}[||,|||]$ carboxylate complexes do not participate in toluene amination chemistry. We have carefully examined the reaction chemistry of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}[\mathsf{I I I}$ ,III] complex ${\sf R u}_{2}(\mathsf{O B}z)_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ with $N a N_{3}$ to better understand the dichotomy between lattice-isolated and homogeneous catalysts $\mathrm{\OBz=}$ benzoate). Treatment of ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O B z})_{4}{\sf C I}$ , or the more soluble analog $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{O B}z)_{4}\mathsf{B F}_{4}$ , with $N a N_{3}$ resulted in rapid one-electron reduction to generate $\mathsf{R u}_{2}[\mathsf{I}$ I,II] complex ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O B z})_{4}$ with concurrent evolution of ${\sf N}_{2}$ (detected by GC, Figure 4). Formation of ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O B z})_{4}$ was indicated by $\mathsf{\Omega}^{1}\mathsf{H}$ NMR, IR, and UVvis spectroscopies (Figures S13, S14, and S15, respectively), and the structure of ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O B z})_{4}$ was confirmed by X-ray diffraction. We hypothesized that the evolution of ${\sf N}_{2}$ proceeds via azide binding, extrusion of one equivalent of ${\sf N}_{2}$ to generate a reactive nitride, and subsequent nitride coupling to generate an additional 0.5 equivalents of ${\sf N}_{2}$ . Consistent with this hypothesis, analysis of the ${\sf N}_{2}$ evolved upon treatment of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{O B}z)_{4}\mathsf{B F}_{4}$ with $\mathsf{N a}^{15}\mathsf{N N}_{2}$ revealed substantial formation of $^{30}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ (Figure S16). Formation of $^{30}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ implies a bimolecular ${\sf N}_{2}$ -forming reaction from mono-labeled $^{15}\mathsf{N N}_{2}^{-}$ . In contrast to the evolution of $^{30}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ from $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{O B}z)_{4}\mathsf{B F}_{4}$ , no $^{30}\mathsf{N}_{2}$ was detected in the headspace following treatment of $\mathsf{R u}_{6}\mathsf{b t c}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}$ with $\mathsf{N a}^{15}\mathsf{N N}_{2}$ .
To gain insight into the selectivity for benzylic versus aromatic $C-H$ amination, we have pursued a series of kinetic isotope effect (KIE) experiments (Figure 5). Amination of $d_{1}$ - toluene proceeds with an intramolecular $k_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathsf{H}}/k_{\mathsf{D}}=7.86(3)$ at 100 $^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure 5a; based on the Arrhenius equation, a $k_{\mathrm{H}}/k_{\mathrm{D}}\sim13.2$
